  "articleBody": "Crisp trim, detailed casing, and tight joints give a home its custom character. If you are hiring a contractor to install new millwork, here is a practical guide to the process.\n\nOverview: Finish carpentry ties together floors, openings, and drywall with baseboards, crown molding, door sets, and custom casing.\n\nKey Benefits: Quality installation conceals structural framing shifts, protects drywall edges from daily wear, and creates crisp architectural lines throughout living spaces.\n\nProcess Breakdown:\n- Confirm millwork profiles, species, wood thicknesses, reveals, and door swing directions.\n- Acclimate wood trim to interior indoor humidity before cutting.\n- Check walls and subfloors for plumb and level, scribing baseboards and jambs where needed.\n- Secure trim solidly into wall studs rather than anchoring solely into drywall.\n- Fill nail penetrations, caulk seams, sand smooth, and complete final primer and paint coats.\n\nReal Examples: In older Denver homes, settling often leaves floors unlevel. Scribing trim cleanly to out-of-level floors prevents ugly caulked wedges beneath baseboards.\n\nWhat to Prepare Before You Start: Decide whether existing trim will be carefully pulled and reused or replaced entirely. HH Remodeling LLC brings expert finish carpentry, door installation, and interior painting directly to your project. Call Abraham Becerra at 303-505-0866 to discuss your trim ideas.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-23"
